      "rev_n_1_7d7b2c_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_1_7d7b2c_0",
        "node_id": "n_1_7d7b2c",
        "thought_text": "Consider sorting the rectangles by their height or width and then iteratively painting them from smallest to largest, ensuring that each rectangle is only painted if it overlaps with already painted areas.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "root",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794412716.245298
      },
      "rev_n_2_42b346_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_2_42b346_0",
        "node_id": "n_2_42b346",
        "thought_text": "Explore a dynamic programming approach where we maintain a 2D array to track the minimum cost to paint up to each cell, updating this array as we process each rectangle.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "root",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794412716.245315
      },
      "rev_n_3_43fcd8_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_3_43fcd8_0",
        "node_id": "n_3_43fcd8",
        "thought_text": "Utilize a greedy algorithm that selects the rectangle with the highest cost-to-benefit ratio (cost divided by the number of new white cells it would produce) and paints it, repeating until all cells are white.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "root",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794412716.2453241
      },
      "rev_n_4_998f6f_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_4_998f6f_0",
        "node_id": "n_4_998f6f",
        "thought_text": "The current approach sorts rectangles by area but does not account for overlapping areas. We need to consider the intersection of rectangles to avoid double-counting painted areas.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "-1545302676157267384",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794412775.009917
      },
      "rev_n_5_efe970_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_5_efe970_0",
        "node_id": "n_5_efe970",
        "thought_text": "Instead of sorting by area, we could sort rectangles by their position or dimensions in a way that prioritizes covering larger uncolored areas first.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "-1545302676157267384",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794412775.0144472
      },
      "rev_n_6_49825a_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_6_49825a_0",
        "node_id": "n_6_49825a",
        "thought_text": "Implement a greedy algorithm that iteratively selects the rectangle that covers the most uncovered area with the least cost per cell, considering overlaps and previously painted areas.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "-1545302676157267384",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794412775.01772
      },
      "rev_n_7_4d5e2c_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_7_4d5e2c_0",
        "node_id": "n_7_4d5e2c",
        "thought_text": "Re-evaluate the sorting criteria for selecting rectangles. Instead of cost-to-benefit ratio, consider a strategy that prioritizes removing the largest overlapping areas first, as this might reduce redundant paint operations.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "-6184007658256401575",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794412804.302643
      },
      "rev_n_8_ef20d5_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_8_ef20d5_0",
        "node_id": "n_8_ef20d5",
        "thought_text": "Implement a dynamic programming approach to keep track of painted cells efficiently, especially considering the large grid size. This could help in minimizing the number of operations by avoiding unnecessary repainting.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "-6184007658256401575",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794412804.302666
      },
      "rev_n_9_8dd6f6_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_9_8dd6f6_0",
        "node_id": "n_9_8dd6f6",
        "thought_text": "Analyze the intersection patterns of the given rectangles to identify minimal sets of rectangles that cover all black cells without overlap, optimizing the total cost based on these sets.",
